.Dd November 11, 2026
.Dt MPUTEST 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm mputest
.Nd check the memory protection map from user mode
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Nm
.Cm residue
.Nm
.Cm guard
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
reads the kernel's report of the Cortex-M0+ memory protection unit through
.Xr sysctl 8
.Pq Va machdep.mpu
and checks it from unprivileged code.
It forks one child per probe address and records whether the child read the
word or died of
.Dv SIGSEGV .
.Pp
Kernel RAM above the process window, kernel text in XIP flash and SIO CPUID
must be closed when
.Va machdep.mpu.enable
reads 1 and open when it reads 0.
Four words of the unstriped SRAM windows at 0x21000000, which name the
process window and kernel RAM one bank at a time, follow the same rule.
The XIP cache-as-SRAM window at 0x15000000 bus-errors while the cache is
enabled, so a read there must kill the child with
.Dv SIGSEGV
in both states; that probe shows the range is closed and does not by itself
show the MPU.
The boot ROM, the top of the process window and the SIO divider status
register must be open in both states, and a float and a double division
through the boot ROM must return the expected bits: the ROM's own divide
routine writes the divider, so a map that closes it faults every division.
.Va machdep.mpu.nregions
must read the datasheet's eight, and enable must be set only when
.Va machdep.mpu.programmed
equals the four regions the kernel's map holds.
.Va machdep.mpu.ctrl
must read 0x5 when enabled and zero when disabled.
.Pp
Each probe prints one line ending in
.Dq ok
or
.Dq FAIL ;
the last line is
.Dq MPUTEST OK
followed by the MPU state, or
.Dq MPUTEST FAIL
and the count of disagreements.
.Pp
.Nm
.Cm residue
checks a second property: the window handed to a new image holds nothing of
the image before it.
It fills the span between the top of this image and the bottom of its stack
with a marker, re-executes itself, and counts the marker words the new image
still reads.
The span is derived from
.Va _end
and
.Dv USER_DATA_END ,
so both images agree on it.
The last line is
.Dq RESIDUE OK
when no word survives and
.Dq RESIDUE LEAK
otherwise.
.Pp
.Nm
.Cm residue
moves the break to the top of that span before planting, because the
break guard below makes the 32 bytes past the rounded break read-only to
the process.
.Pp
.Nm
.Cm guard
checks the break guard, MPU region 7, which covers the 32 bytes at the
break rounded up to 32 and admits user loads but not user stores there
while the stack stays at least 1,056 bytes above it.
It predicts
.Va machdep.mpu.guardbase
and
.Va machdep.mpu.guardattr
from
.Xr sbrk 2 ,
then checks in children that a store at the guard base dies of
.Dv SIGSEGV ,
a load there and stores below it and 32 bytes above it succeed, the guard
moves with
.Fn sbrk 64 ,
a
.Dv SIGSEGV
handler that grows the break lets the replayed store complete, a recursion
that makes a system call in every frame reaches the break with the guard off,
a stack that descends without a system call and spins for several clock ticks
near the guard then descends through it, and a stack pointer moved into the
guard by one instruction is killed with
.Dv SIGKILL .
The last line is
.Dq GUARD OK
followed by the MPU state, or
.Dq GUARD FAIL
and the count of disagreements.
With the MPU off every store succeeds, the guard reads disabled and the
.Dv SIGKILL
probe is skipped.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
The number of probes that disagreed with the kernel's report, or 2 when
.Xr sysctl 3
or
.Xr fork 2
failed.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr sysctl 8
.Sh CAVEATS
Each closed probe under an enabled MPU is a real HardFault, and the kernel
prints its fault frame on the console before delivering the signal.
